column408:
 the two address can be different. You cannot directly write to `0x42000000` without triggering a fault.

What you have to do is always using the address returned by `syscon_get_base()` and nothing else. You can write a certain amount of bytes at that address with direct memory access and then you should try to get back using the sysycon driver. Or the opposite: you use the syscon driver to write some data and retrieve that back using direct memory access. Bottom line: you never access directly the memory using the physical address.

This is of course valid if you have the MMU enabled (as you should if you are using A53)."}

